window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["langchain-level-up"]=[{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0001","card":"q-langchain-level-up-001-1-overview-prerequisites-setup","sec":"langchain-level-up-s002","q":"What problem does RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) solve for LLM applications?","a":"<p>An LLM only knows its training data. It has a knowledge cut-off, no access to your private documents, and little domain-specific knowledge, so it can answer inaccurately or make things up. RAG retrieves relevant external data at query time and adds it to the prompt, so the answer is grounded in current, specific information. The trade-off is extra moving parts: an index, a retriever, and added latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0002","card":"q-langchain-level-up-001-1-overview-prerequisites-setup","sec":"langchain-level-up-s002","q":"Why should an OpenAI API key be kept in a <code>.env</code> file loaded with <code>python-dotenv</code> instead of being written in the code?","a":"<p>Keys in source code leak through version control and logs. A <code>.env</code> file stays out of git (listed in <code>.gitignore</code>), and <code>load_dotenv()</code> puts its values into environment variables at runtime. In <code>langchain-openai</code>, <code>ChatOpenAI()</code> and <code>OpenAIEmbeddings()</code> read <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code> from the environment automatically, so no key appears in code. In hosted deployments you set the same variable in the platform's secret settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0003","card":"q-langchain-level-up-001-1-overview-prerequisites-setup","sec":"langchain-level-up-s002","q":"Why do you create a Python virtual environment (<code>python3 -m venv venv</code>) before running <code>pip install</code> for a LangChain project?","a":"<p>It isolates the project's dependencies from the system Python and from other projects. LangChain packages change often and pin different versions of <code>pydantic</code> and <code>openai</code>, so a shared environment easily produces version conflicts. A <code>requirements.txt</code> then makes the setup reproducible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0004","card":"q-langchain-level-up-001-1-overview-prerequisites-setup","sec":"langchain-level-up-s002","q":"In LangChain 0.1+, the framework is split into several packages. What do <code>langchain-core</code>, <code>langchain-openai</code> and <code>langchain-community</code> each hold?","a":"<p><code>langchain-core</code> holds the base abstractions: runnables, prompts, messages, output parsers, and the LCEL <code>|</code> operator. <code>langchain-openai</code> holds the OpenAI integrations such as <code>ChatOpenAI</code> and <code>OpenAIEmbeddings</code>. <code>langchain-community</code> holds third-party integrations such as document loaders and vector stores like FAISS and Chroma. The split keeps the core small and lets each integration be versioned separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0005","card":"q-langchain-level-up-002-2-langchain-basics-model-i-o-prompt-temp","sec":"langchain-level-up-s003","q":"What are the three stages of the \"model I/O\" flow in LangChain?","a":"<p>Format the input (a prompt template fills variables into instructions), call the model, then parse the model's output into a usable shape. A chain is a sequence of such components that passes each one's output to the next.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0006","card":"q-langchain-level-up-002-2-langchain-basics-model-i-o-prompt-temp","sec":"langchain-level-up-s003","q":"Why use a chain of separate components (prompt, model, parser) instead of one function that builds a string and calls the model?","a":"<p>Each part is reusable and replaceable. You can swap the model, change the prompt, or add a parser without rewriting the rest. All parts share the Runnable interface, so the chain gets <code>invoke</code>, <code>batch</code>, <code>stream</code> and async variants without extra code. The cost is a small learning curve and harder-to-read tracebacks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0007","card":"q-langchain-level-up-003-2-1-first-application-llm-invoke","sec":"langchain-level-up-s004","q":"In <code>langchain_openai</code>, what is the difference between <code>OpenAI</code> and <code>ChatOpenAI</code>?","a":"<p><code>OpenAI</code> wraps the older text-completion style LLM interface: it takes a string and returns a string. <code>ChatOpenAI</code> wraps chat models: it takes a list of messages (or a string converted to one) and returns an <code>AIMessage</code>. Most current OpenAI models are chat models, so <code>ChatOpenAI</code> is the usual choice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0008","card":"q-langchain-level-up-003-2-1-first-application-llm-invoke","sec":"langchain-level-up-s004","q":"What does <code>llm.invoke(\"Tell me a joke\")</code> return for a <code>ChatOpenAI</code> model versus an <code>OpenAI</code> (completion) model?","a":"<p>A <code>ChatOpenAI</code> model returns an <code>AIMessage</code>; the text is in <code>.content</code> and metadata such as token usage is attached. The completion-style <code>OpenAI</code> class returns a plain <code>str</code>. This difference is why a <code>StrOutputParser</code> is often added to chat chains.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0009","card":"q-langchain-level-up-003-2-1-first-application-llm-invoke","sec":"langchain-level-up-s004","q":"Why does LangChain give every LLM, prompt, retriever and parser the same <code>invoke</code> method?","a":"<p>They all implement the Runnable interface. One uniform method means any component can be composed with any other, and a chain is itself a Runnable, so it can be invoked, batched, streamed or nested inside another chain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0010","card":"q-langchain-level-up-004-2-2-prompt-templates","sec":"langchain-level-up-s005","q":"What is a <code>PromptTemplate</code> and why use it instead of an f-string?","a":"<p>A <code>PromptTemplate</code> is a reusable prompt with named <code>{variable}</code> placeholders. It validates that the variables are supplied, can be composed in an LCEL chain, can be serialized and versioned, and works with tracing tools such as LangSmith. An f-string is fine for a one-off, but it cannot be plugged into a chain as a Runnable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0011","card":"q-langchain-level-up-004-2-2-prompt-templates","sec":"langchain-level-up-s005","q":"What does this code print?","a":"<p>It prints <code>Name a company that makes socks?</code>. <code>format</code> substitutes the variable and returns a plain string; no model is called. In a chain you would use <code>invoke</code> and get a <code>StringPromptValue</code> instead.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from langchain_core.prompts import PromptTemplate\n\nprompt = PromptTemplate.from_template(&quot;Name a company that makes {product}?&quot;)\nprint(prompt.format(product=&quot;socks&quot;))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0012","card":"q-langchain-level-up-004-2-2-prompt-templates","sec":"langchain-level-up-s005","q":"What happens if you call <code>prompt.format()</code> on a template with a <code>{topic}</code> placeholder without passing <code>topic</code>?","a":"<p>It raises a <code>KeyError</code> for the missing variable. The template needs every input variable. The list of expected variables is available in <code>prompt.input_variables</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0013","card":"q-langchain-level-up-004-2-2-prompt-templates","sec":"langchain-level-up-s005","q":"When do you choose <code>ChatPromptTemplate</code> over <code>PromptTemplate</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>ChatPromptTemplate</code> when the model is a chat model and you need distinct roles (system, human, AI) or message history. It builds a list of messages. <code>PromptTemplate</code> builds a single string, which is enough for simple completion-style prompts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0014","card":"q-langchain-level-up-004-2-2-prompt-templates","sec":"langchain-level-up-s005","q":"In LangChain 0.1+, where should <code>PromptTemplate</code> be imported from, <code>langchain.prompts</code> or <code>langchain_core.prompts</code>?","a":"<p>The canonical location is <code>langchain_core.prompts</code>. In LangChain 0.1/0.2, <code>langchain.prompts</code> still worked as a re-export, so older tutorials use it. New code should import from <code>langchain_core.prompts</code>, because the re-exports are a legacy path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0015","card":"q-langchain-level-up-005-2-3-lcel-binding-prompt-model-with","sec":"langchain-level-up-s006","q":"What is LCEL and what does the <code>|</code> operator do?","a":"<p>LCEL (LangChain Expression Language) is a declarative way to compose Runnables. <code>a | b</code> creates a <code>RunnableSequence</code> where the output of <code>a</code> becomes the input of <code>b</code>. The result is itself a Runnable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0016","card":"q-langchain-level-up-005-2-3-lcel-binding-prompt-model-with","sec":"langchain-level-up-s006","q":"Why must you pass a dict such as <code>chain.invoke({\"topic\": \"cats\"})</code> to a chain that starts with a prompt template?","a":"<p>The first step is the prompt template, and it expects a mapping from its variable names to values. The dict keys must match the template's placeholders. A bare string fails with an error. If you want to pass a plain string, put a step first that builds the dict, such as a dict of Runnables with <code>RunnablePassthrough()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0017","card":"q-langchain-level-up-005-2-3-lcel-binding-prompt-model-with","sec":"langchain-level-up-s006","q":"What benefits does LCEL give you over the legacy <code>LLMChain</code> class?","a":"<p>In LangChain 0.1/0.2+, LCEL chains support streaming, async, batch and parallel execution out of the box, show every step in tracing, and are easier to customize because each step is explicit. Legacy classes like <code>LLMChain</code> were deprecated in favor of LCEL. The trade-off is that the <code>|</code> syntax can feel less obvious to read at first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0018","card":"q-langchain-level-up-005-2-3-lcel-binding-prompt-model-with","sec":"langchain-level-up-s006","q":"How do you run several independent steps in parallel inside an LCEL chain?","a":"<p>Use a dict (which LCEL coerces to a <code>RunnableParallel</code>) or <code>RunnableParallel</code> directly. Each value is a Runnable that receives the same input, and the result is a dict of their outputs. This is how a RAG chain fills <code>context</code> from a retriever and <code>question</code> from the input at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0019","card":"q-langchain-level-up-006-2-4-output-parsers","sec":"langchain-level-up-s007","q":"Why add <code>StrOutputParser()</code> as the last step of a chat model chain?","a":"<p>A chat model returns an <code>AIMessage</code> with content and metadata. <code>StrOutputParser</code> extracts the text and returns a plain <code>str</code>, so downstream code, printing, or a UI gets a string. Without it you must read <code>.content</code> yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0020","card":"q-langchain-level-up-006-2-4-output-parsers","sec":"langchain-level-up-s007","q":"Why does the output parser go last in an LCEL chain and not between the prompt and the model?","a":"<p>Order follows data flow: prompt produces a prompt value, the model consumes it and produces a message, and the parser consumes that message. A parser placed before the model would receive a prompt value, which is the wrong input type.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0021","card":"q-langchain-level-up-006-2-4-output-parsers","sec":"langchain-level-up-s007","q":"When would you use <code>with_structured_output</code> or a Pydantic-based parser instead of <code>StrOutputParser</code>?","a":"<p>When the application needs typed, machine-readable data (a schema with fields) rather than free text, for example for routing or extraction. <code>StrOutputParser</code> only returns text. Structured output makes the model return fields that match a schema, which is far more reliable than parsing free text with regex.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0022","card":"q-langchain-level-up-007-3-embeddings-vector-store-faiss-retrieve","sec":"langchain-level-up-s008","q":"What is an embedding and why does it enable semantic search?","a":"<p>An embedding is a vector of floats that represents the meaning of a text. Texts with similar meaning land close together in vector space, so comparing the query vector with stored vectors (cosine similarity or distance) finds relevant content even when the words differ. Keyword search cannot do that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0023","card":"q-langchain-level-up-007-3-embeddings-vector-store-faiss-retrieve","sec":"langchain-level-up-s008","q":"How does a vector store answer a query?","a":"<p>It embeds the query with the same embedding model used for the documents, compares it with the stored vectors, and returns the top-k closest documents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0024","card":"q-langchain-level-up-007-3-embeddings-vector-store-faiss-retrieve","sec":"langchain-level-up-s008","q":"What is the difference between <code>vector_store.similarity_search(query, k=1)</code> and <code>vector_store.as_retriever()</code>?","a":"<p><code>similarity_search</code> is a direct method on the store that returns a list of <code>Document</code> objects. <code>as_retriever()</code> wraps the store as a Retriever, which is a Runnable, so it can be placed in an LCEL chain. The retriever also carries search settings such as <code>search_type</code> and <code>search_kwargs</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0025","card":"q-langchain-level-up-007-3-embeddings-vector-store-faiss-retrieve","sec":"langchain-level-up-s008","q":"What does the <code>k</code> parameter control in a similarity search, and what is the trade-off of a larger value?","a":"<p><code>k</code> is the number of top results returned. A larger <code>k</code> raises the chance that the relevant chunk is included, but it adds noise and tokens to the prompt, which raises cost and can distract the model. A smaller <code>k</code> is cheaper but may miss the answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0026","card":"q-langchain-level-up-007-3-embeddings-vector-store-faiss-retrieve","sec":"langchain-level-up-s008","q":"In this LCEL RAG chain, why is <code>RunnablePassthrough()</code> used as the value for <code>\"question\"</code>?","a":"<p>The dict runs both entries in parallel on the same input string. The retriever turns the string into documents for <code>context</code>, and <code>RunnablePassthrough()</code> forwards the original string unchanged for <code>question</code>. The prompt then has both variables it needs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>chain = (\n    {&quot;context&quot;: retriever, &quot;question&quot;: RunnablePassthrough()}\n    | prompt\n    | llm\n    | StrOutputParser()\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0027","card":"q-langchain-level-up-007-3-embeddings-vector-store-faiss-retrieve","sec":"langchain-level-up-s008","q":"What does a LangChain <code>Document</code> contain?","a":"<p>Two fields: <code>page_content</code> (the text) and <code>metadata</code> (a dict such as source, page number, or any custom tags). Metadata can be used for filtering and for citing where an answer came from.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0028","card":"q-langchain-level-up-007-3-embeddings-vector-store-faiss-retrieve","sec":"langchain-level-up-s008","q":"What are FAISS and when would you pick it?","a":"<p>FAISS is a library from Meta for fast similarity search over dense vectors. In LangChain it is typically used in memory, which makes it quick to set up for prototypes and small to mid-size data. It does not give you a managed database with auth, replication or metadata querying, so for production you often choose a hosted vector database.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0029","card":"q-langchain-level-up-007-3-embeddings-vector-store-faiss-retrieve","sec":"langchain-level-up-s008","q":"Which package is needed to use the FAISS vector store from LangChain, and where does the LangChain wrapper live?","a":"<p>Install <code>faiss-cpu</code> (or <code>faiss-gpu</code>) for the library. In LangChain 0.1+, the wrapper <code>FAISS</code> is imported from <code>langchain_community.vectorstores</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0030","card":"q-langchain-level-up-008-4-1-why-rag-architecture","sec":"langchain-level-up-s009","q":"Name the limits of an LLM that RAG addresses.","a":"<p>The training-data cut-off (no recent facts), missing specialized domain knowledge, and no access to private data such as internal policies. RAG supplies that information at query time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0031","card":"q-langchain-level-up-008-4-1-why-rag-architecture","sec":"langchain-level-up-s009","q":"Walk through the stages of a RAG pipeline in order.","a":"<p>Indexing (offline): load documents, split into chunks, embed the chunks, store the vectors. Query time: embed the user query, retrieve the most similar chunks, combine them with the question in the prompt, and let the LLM generate the answer from that context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0032","card":"q-langchain-level-up-008-4-1-why-rag-architecture","sec":"langchain-level-up-s009","q":"When would you choose RAG over fine-tuning to give a model company knowledge?","a":"<p>RAG is better when the data changes often, when you need citations, or when you must keep private data out of model weights. You update the index instead of retraining. Fine-tuning is better for changing style, format or behavior, and it is a poor way to inject facts that must stay current. Many systems combine both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0033","card":"q-langchain-level-up-008-4-1-why-rag-architecture","sec":"langchain-level-up-s009","q":"What are the main drawbacks or failure modes of RAG?","a":"<p>Retrieval can miss the right chunk (bad chunking, weak embeddings, wrong <code>k</code>), irrelevant chunks can mislead the model, the extra retrieval step adds latency and cost, and the context window limits how much can be injected. Quality depends on the retriever as much as on the LLM, so you need to evaluate retrieval separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0034","card":"q-langchain-level-up-008-4-1-why-rag-architecture","sec":"langchain-level-up-s009","q":"A RAG chatbot gives a wrong answer. How do you work out whether retrieval or generation is at fault?","a":"<p>Inspect the retrieved chunks for that question. If the right text is not among them, fix retrieval: chunk size and overlap, embedding model, <code>k</code>, or query rewriting. If the right text is present but the answer is wrong, fix the prompt (grounding instructions) or the model. Tracing tools such as LangSmith show the retrieved documents for each run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0035","card":"q-langchain-level-up-009-4-2-load-and-split-documents","sec":"langchain-level-up-s010","q":"Why must documents be split into chunks before embedding?","a":"<p>A whole document produces one vector that blurs many topics, and it may exceed the embedding model's input limit. Smaller chunks give focused vectors, so retrieval returns only the relevant part and keeps the prompt short. Chunks that are too small lose context, so size is a trade-off.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0036","card":"q-langchain-level-up-009-4-2-load-and-split-documents","sec":"langchain-level-up-s010","q":"In a LangChain text splitter, what do <code>chunk_size</code> and <code>chunk_overlap</code> control?","a":"<p><code>chunk_size</code> is the maximum length of a chunk (in characters by default). <code>chunk_overlap</code> is how much text adjacent chunks share. Overlap keeps a sentence or idea that falls on a boundary available in both chunks, at the price of redundancy and a larger index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0037","card":"q-langchain-level-up-009-4-2-load-and-split-documents","sec":"langchain-level-up-s010","q":"What is wrong or risky about this splitter configuration for a document of long paragraphs?","a":"<p><code>CharacterTextSplitter</code> splits only on the one separator, and then merges pieces up to <code>chunk_size</code>. A sentence longer than 100 characters stays in one chunk that exceeds the limit (LangChain only warns). With zero overlap, an idea that spans two sentences can be cut apart. <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code> is usually the better default.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>splitter = CharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=100, chunk_overlap=0, separator=&quot;.&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0038","card":"q-langchain-level-up-009-4-2-load-and-split-documents","sec":"langchain-level-up-s010","q":"What is the difference between <code>CharacterTextSplitter</code> and <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code>?","a":"<p><code>CharacterTextSplitter</code> splits on a single separator. <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code> tries a list of separators in order (paragraphs, lines, spaces, characters) and recurses until the pieces fit <code>chunk_size</code>, so it keeps paragraphs and sentences together where possible. It is the recommended default for generic text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0039","card":"q-langchain-level-up-009-4-2-load-and-split-documents","sec":"langchain-level-up-s010","q":"What does <code>TextLoader(\"file.txt\").load()</code> return, and how does <code>split_documents</code> use it?","a":"<p><code>load()</code> returns a list of <code>Document</code> objects (one for a whole text file) with the text in <code>page_content</code> and the file path in <code>metadata</code>. <code>split_documents</code> takes that list and returns a new list of smaller <code>Document</code> objects, each keeping the metadata. Use <code>split_text</code> instead when you have a raw string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0040","card":"q-langchain-level-up-009-4-2-load-and-split-documents","sec":"langchain-level-up-s010","q":"Which loader would you use for a PDF, and which for a web page?","a":"<p><code>PyPDFLoader</code> for a PDF (one <code>Document</code> per page, with page number in metadata), and <code>WebBaseLoader</code> for a web page (it fetches the HTML and extracts text). Both live in <code>langchain_community.document_loaders</code> in LangChain 0.1+.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0041","card":"q-langchain-level-up-010-4-3-embeddings-and-chroma-vector-store","sec":"langchain-level-up-s011","q":"How do you interpret distance between two embeddings?","a":"<p>A small distance (or high cosine similarity) means the texts are semantically related, a large distance means they are not. The raw numbers are meaningful only relative to each other for the same embedding model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0042","card":"q-langchain-level-up-010-4-3-embeddings-and-chroma-vector-store","sec":"langchain-level-up-s011","q":"What does <code>Chroma.from_documents(chunks, embeddings)</code> do in one call?","a":"<p>It embeds every chunk with the given embedding model and stores the vectors with their text and metadata in a Chroma collection, returning a vector store you can search or turn into a retriever.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0043","card":"q-langchain-level-up-010-4-3-embeddings-and-chroma-vector-store","sec":"langchain-level-up-s011","q":"What vector sizes do OpenAI's <code>text-embedding-ada-002</code>, <code>text-embedding-3-small</code> and <code>text-embedding-3-large</code> output by default?","a":"<p><code>text-embedding-ada-002</code> and <code>text-embedding-3-small</code> output 1536 dimensions, and <code>text-embedding-3-large</code> outputs 3072. The <code>text-embedding-3</code> models can return shorter vectors through a <code>dimensions</code> parameter. Larger vectors can capture more nuance but cost more storage and search time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0044","card":"q-langchain-level-up-010-4-3-embeddings-and-chroma-vector-store","sec":"langchain-level-up-s011","q":"Why must the same embedding model be used at indexing time and at query time?","a":"<p>Each model has its own vector space. Vectors from two models are not comparable and can even have different dimensions. Changing the embedding model means re-embedding and rebuilding the whole index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0045","card":"q-langchain-level-up-011-4-4-chain-without-vs-with-context","sec":"langchain-level-up-s012","q":"In a chat prompt for RAG, where do you put the retrieved <code>{context}</code> and where the user question, and why?","a":"<p>Put <code>{context}</code> in the system message (with the instruction to answer from it) and the user question in the human message. The system role sets the rules and grounding material, and the human role carries the question. This keeps the instructions separate from the user input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0046","card":"q-langchain-level-up-011-4-4-chain-without-vs-with-context","sec":"langchain-level-up-s012","q":"What is the behavior difference between asking the same question with and without retrieved context?","a":"<p>Without context, the model answers from its general training and gives a generic or wrong answer about private data. With retrieved context, it answers from your data and gives a specific, accurate reply. This is the core benefit of RAG.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0047","card":"q-langchain-level-up-011-4-4-chain-without-vs-with-context","sec":"langchain-level-up-s012","q":"Why does the RAG chain's input become a plain string (<code>chain.invoke(\"What is the return policy?\")</code>) while the no-context chain takes a dict (<code>{\"user_query\": ...}</code>)?","a":"<p>In the RAG chain, the first step is a dict of Runnables (<code>retriever</code> and <code>RunnablePassthrough()</code>), both of which accept the string and build the dict the prompt needs. The no-context chain starts directly at the prompt, which needs a dict keyed by its variable names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0048","card":"q-langchain-level-up-011-4-4-chain-without-vs-with-context","sec":"langchain-level-up-s012","q":"How do you stop a RAG chatbot from inventing an answer when the retrieved context does not contain it?","a":"<p>Add an explicit instruction in the prompt: answer only from the context, and say you do not know if the answer is not there. This lowers hallucination but does not remove it, so combine it with good retrieval and evaluation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0049","card":"q-langchain-level-up-011-4-4-chain-without-vs-with-context","sec":"langchain-level-up-s012","q":"What does <code>SystemMessagePromptTemplate.from_template(...)</code> plus <code>HumanMessagePromptTemplate</code> achieve compared with <code>ChatPromptTemplate.from_messages([(\"system\", ...), (\"human\", ...)])</code>?","a":"<p>They build the same kind of chat prompt. The explicit classes give you message-template objects, while the tuple form is the shorter, now more common style. Both end up as a <code>ChatPromptTemplate</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0050","card":"q-langchain-level-up-012-4-5-complete-rag-function","sec":"langchain-level-up-s013","q":"How would you structure a small RAG app into functions?","a":"<p>Use one function to load and split documents, one to embed and store them and return a retriever, and one to build the chain and answer a query. Build the index once at startup and reuse it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0051","card":"q-langchain-level-up-012-4-5-complete-rag-function","sec":"langchain-level-up-s013","q":"Why do you pass the retriever into the answer function instead of creating it inside?","a":"<p>It separates indexing from querying, makes the function easy to test with a fake retriever, and avoids rebuilding the vector store on each request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0052","card":"q-langchain-level-up-013-4-6-challenge-context-and-history-aware","sec":"langchain-level-up-s014","q":"Why does a plain RAG chain fail on follow-up questions like \"What about its cost?\"","a":"<p>The retriever only sees that one message, which has no subject, so it fetches irrelevant chunks. A history-aware step first rewrites the follow-up into a standalone question using the chat history, and only then retrieves.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0053","card":"q-langchain-level-up-013-4-6-challenge-context-and-history-aware","sec":"langchain-level-up-s014","q":"In LangChain 0.1/0.2+, what does <code>create_history_aware_retriever(llm, retriever, prompt)</code> do?","a":"<p>It returns a retriever that, when chat history exists, asks the LLM (with the given prompt) to rewrite the latest input into a standalone question, then passes that question to the base retriever. If there is no history, it passes the input straight to the retriever.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0054","card":"q-langchain-level-up-013-4-6-challenge-context-and-history-aware","sec":"langchain-level-up-s014","q":"What does <code>create_stuff_documents_chain(llm, prompt)</code> do, and what is the limit of the \"stuff\" approach?","a":"<p>It formats all retrieved documents into the prompt's <code>{context}</code> variable and calls the LLM once. It is simple and uses one LLM call, but all documents must fit in the context window. For many or long documents you need other strategies such as map-reduce or refine.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0055","card":"q-langchain-level-up-013-4-6-challenge-context-and-history-aware","sec":"langchain-level-up-s014","q":"What does <code>create_retrieval_chain(history_aware_retriever, question_answer_chain)</code> return when invoked?","a":"<p>A dict that includes the input, the retrieved <code>context</code> documents, and the model's <code>answer</code>. This is why code reads <code>response[\"answer\"]</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0056","card":"q-langchain-level-up-013-4-6-challenge-context-and-history-aware","sec":"langchain-level-up-s014","q":"In this chat prompt, what is the role of <code>MessagesPlaceholder(\"chat_history\")</code>?","a":"<p>It reserves a slot in the prompt where a list of previous messages (<code>HumanMessage</code>, <code>AIMessage</code>) is inserted at run time. It sits between the system message and the latest human input so the model sees the conversation in order.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>qa_prompt = ChatPromptTemplate.from_messages([\n    (&quot;system&quot;, &quot;Use the context to answer. {context}&quot;),\n    MessagesPlaceholder(&quot;chat_history&quot;),\n    (&quot;human&quot;, &quot;{input}&quot;),\n])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0057","card":"q-langchain-level-up-013-4-6-challenge-context-and-history-aware","sec":"langchain-level-up-s014","q":"In a RAG chain with history, why must the chat history be updated after every answer, and what goes in it?","a":"<p>The chain itself is stateless. The app must append the user's <code>HumanMessage</code> and the model's <code>AIMessage</code> after each turn and pass the list on the next call. Without that, the model cannot resolve follow-ups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0058","card":"q-langchain-level-up-013-4-6-challenge-context-and-history-aware","sec":"langchain-level-up-s014","q":"What is the drawback of keeping the whole chat history in a Python list and sending it every time?","a":"<p>The prompt grows with each turn, which raises cost and latency and will eventually exceed the context window. Fix it by trimming, summarizing older turns, or keeping only the last N messages. A global list also mixes users in a multi-user app, so keep a history per session.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0059","card":"q-langchain-level-up-013-4-6-challenge-context-and-history-aware","sec":"langchain-level-up-s014","q":"In the history-aware setup, why do the rewrite prompt and the answer prompt differ?","a":"<p>The rewrite prompt must only turn the follow-up into a standalone question and not answer it, so it has no <code>{context}</code>. The answer prompt receives the retrieved documents in <code>{context}</code> and tells the model to answer from them. They have different jobs and different inputs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0060","card":"q-langchain-level-up-014-5-interactive-web-app-with-streamlit","sec":"langchain-level-up-s015","q":"What is Streamlit and why is it popular for GenAI prototypes?","a":"<p>Streamlit is an open-source Python library that turns a script into an interactive web app without HTML or JavaScript. It has built-in chat components, reruns on interaction, and simple deployment, so you can put a UI on an LLM app in minutes. It is less suited to complex custom front-ends and high-traffic production sites.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0061","card":"q-langchain-level-up-014-5-interactive-web-app-with-streamlit","sec":"langchain-level-up-s015","q":"Streamlit reruns the whole script on every user interaction. What is wrong with this code if you want the chat history to survive reruns?","a":"<p><code>messages</code> is a normal variable and is recreated as an empty list on every rerun. Use <code>st.session_state</code>, initializing it once with <code>if \"messages\" not in st.session_state: st.session_state.messages = []</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import streamlit as st\n\nmessages = []\nprompt = st.chat_input(&quot;What&#x27;s up?&quot;)\nif prompt:\n    messages.append({&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: prompt})</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0062","card":"q-langchain-level-up-014-5-interactive-web-app-with-streamlit","sec":"langchain-level-up-s015","q":"What do <code>st.chat_input</code> and <code>st.chat_message</code> do?","a":"<p><code>st.chat_input</code> shows a chat box fixed at the bottom of the page and returns the submitted text (or <code>None</code> if nothing was submitted). <code>st.chat_message(role)</code> creates a message bubble for the given role (<code>\"user\"</code> or <code>\"assistant\"</code>), and you write content inside it with a <code>with</code> block.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0063","card":"q-langchain-level-up-014-5-interactive-web-app-with-streamlit","sec":"langchain-level-up-s015","q":"How do you connect a RAG function to a Streamlit chat UI?","a":"<p>Keep the RAG logic in a separate module (a <code>query(text)</code> function). In the app, read the text from <code>st.chat_input</code>, call the function, show both messages with <code>st.chat_message</code>, and append both to <code>st.session_state.messages</code>. Separating the logic from the UI keeps it testable and reusable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0064","card":"q-langchain-level-up-014-5-interactive-web-app-with-streamlit","sec":"langchain-level-up-s015","q":"How do you launch a Streamlit app locally?","a":"<p>Run <code>streamlit run app.py</code>. It starts a local server, by default on port 8501, and opens the browser.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0065","card":"q-langchain-level-up-014-5-interactive-web-app-with-streamlit","sec":"langchain-level-up-s015","q":"Why is the system message stored in <code>st.session_state.messages</code> but skipped when rendering?","a":"<p>It sets the assistant's behavior and belongs in the history sent to the model, but the user should not see it. The render loop skips entries whose role is <code>system</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0066","card":"q-langchain-level-up-014-5-interactive-web-app-with-streamlit","sec":"langchain-level-up-s015","q":"When a Streamlit LLM app is slow because it rebuilds the vector store on every rerun, what do you do?","a":"<p>Move the expensive setup into a function cached with <code>st.cache_resource</code> (or keep the result in <code>st.session_state</code>) so it runs once per process, not on each interaction.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0067","card":"q-langchain-level-up-015-deploying-to-streamlit-community-cloud","sec":"langchain-level-up-s016","q":"Why must <code>.env</code> be in <code>.gitignore</code> before pushing an app to GitHub for Streamlit Community Cloud?","a":"<p>Pushing it would publish your API keys. Anyone can find leaked keys and spend your credits. If a key was ever committed, revoke and rotate it, because deleting the file later does not remove it from git history.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0068","card":"q-langchain-level-up-015-deploying-to-streamlit-community-cloud","sec":"langchain-level-up-s016","q":"How do you give a deployed Streamlit app its OpenAI key when <code>.env</code> is not in the repository?","a":"<p>Add it in the app's Secrets settings (TOML format) in Streamlit Community Cloud, and read it in code with <code>st.secrets[\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"]</code>. Then pass it explicitly, for example <code>ChatOpenAI(api_key=st.secrets[\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"])</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0069","card":"q-langchain-level-up-015-deploying-to-streamlit-community-cloud","sec":"langchain-level-up-s016","q":"What files must the repository contain for Streamlit Community Cloud to run the app?","a":"<p>The app entry script (for example <code>app.py</code>), any modules it imports, data files it reads, and a <code>requirements.txt</code> that lists dependencies. Missing entries in <code>requirements.txt</code> are the most common cause of import errors on deploy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0070","card":"q-langchain-level-up-015-deploying-to-streamlit-community-cloud","sec":"langchain-level-up-s016","q":"What does <code>st.secrets</code> do locally?","a":"<p>Locally it reads from <code>.streamlit/secrets.toml</code> in the project. That file must also be git-ignored, since it holds real keys.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0071","card":"q-langchain-level-up-016-6-q-a-agent-with-multiple-data-sources-q","sec":"langchain-level-up-s017","q":"What is query analysis in an LLM application?","a":"<p>It is a step before retrieval where an LLM inspects the user question and produces structured output, such as the rewritten search query and which data source or filter to use. The structured result then drives retrieval.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0072","card":"q-langchain-level-up-016-6-q-a-agent-with-multiple-data-sources-q","sec":"langchain-level-up-s017","q":"Why use query analysis and routing when you have several data sets?","a":"<p>Searching one combined index mixes unrelated content and lowers precision. Routing the question to the right retriever gives more relevant context and lets each source have its own index, settings, or access rules. The cost is an extra LLM call and the risk of routing to the wrong source.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0073","card":"q-langchain-level-up-016-6-q-a-agent-with-multiple-data-sources-q","sec":"langchain-level-up-s017","q":"What two technologies make structured query analysis work with an OpenAI model?","a":"<p>Function (tool) calling, which makes the model return arguments that match a schema, and a Pydantic model that defines that schema, including field descriptions the model reads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0074","card":"q-langchain-level-up-016-6-q-a-agent-with-multiple-data-sources-q","sec":"langchain-level-up-s017","q":"When would you use query analysis and when would plain RAG over one index be enough?","a":"<p>Use it when questions may need different data stores, filters, or sub-queries, such as separate product categories or per-user data. For one homogeneous document set, plain RAG is simpler, faster and cheaper.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0075","card":"q-langchain-level-up-017-6-1-query-analyzer","sec":"langchain-level-up-s018","q":"In this schema, why does each field have a <code>Field(description=...)</code>?","a":"<p>The descriptions are sent to the model as part of the function/tool schema. The model uses them to decide what to put in each field, so clear descriptions improve the output. Vague descriptions give poor routing.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nclass Search(BaseModel):\n    query: str = Field(description=&quot;Question asked by the user&quot;)\n    person: str = Field(description=&quot;Person targeted in the user query&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0076","card":"q-langchain-level-up-017-6-1-query-analyzer","sec":"langchain-level-up-s018","q":"What does <code>llm.with_structured_output(Search)</code> return, and what type does invoking it give?","a":"<p>It returns a new Runnable that calls the model with the schema and parses the result. Invoking it gives an instance of the <code>Search</code> Pydantic class (or a dict if you pass a dict schema), not text. It works with models that support tool/function calling, for example <code>ChatOpenAI</code> in LangChain 0.1+.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0077","card":"q-langchain-level-up-017-6-1-query-analyzer","sec":"langchain-level-up-s018","q":"What would you do if the query analyzer returns a value outside the allowed set, such as a category that does not exist?","a":"<p>Constrain the schema (a <code>Literal[...]</code> type or an enum) so only valid values are allowed, describe the options in the prompt, and handle a failure path (default retriever or a clarifying question) instead of indexing a dict blindly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0078","card":"q-langchain-level-up-017-6-1-query-analyzer","sec":"langchain-level-up-s018","q":"Why set <code>temperature=0</code> for the model used in a query analyzer?","a":"<p>Routing and extraction should be deterministic and repeatable. Randomness adds no value and can make the same question route differently between runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0079","card":"q-langchain-level-up-017-6-1-query-analyzer","sec":"langchain-level-up-s018","q":"How does <code>with_structured_output</code> differ from using a <code>PydanticOutputParser</code> with format instructions in the prompt?","a":"<p><code>with_structured_output</code> uses the model's native tool/function calling, so the model is constrained to the schema and no format text goes in the prompt. A parser approach asks the model through prompt text to emit JSON and then parses it, which fails more often on malformed output. Native tool calling is preferred when the model supports it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0080","card":"q-langchain-level-up-018-6-2-routing-to-a-retriever-with-a-custom","sec":"langchain-level-up-s019","q":"What does the <code>@chain</code> decorator do in <code>langchain_core.runnables</code>?","a":"<p>It turns a plain Python function into a Runnable, so it can be called with <code>invoke</code>, piped with <code>|</code>, and traced like other components. It is the decorator form of <code>RunnableLambda</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0081","card":"q-langchain-level-up-018-6-2-routing-to-a-retriever-with-a-custom","sec":"langchain-level-up-s019","q":"Why use a custom function with <code>@chain</code> for routing instead of putting everything into a plain <code>|</code> pipeline?","a":"<p>Routing needs ordinary Python control flow: choose a retriever based on a value in the analyzer's output. A <code>|</code> pipeline has a fixed shape, while inside a function you can branch, look up a dict, or handle errors, and nested Runnables are still traced.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0082","card":"q-langchain-level-up-018-6-2-routing-to-a-retriever-with-a-custom","sec":"langchain-level-up-s019","q":"What does this routing function do, and what happens if the analyzer returns a key that is not in the dict?","a":"<p>It analyzes the question, picks a retriever by the <code>person</code> field, and retrieves documents with the rewritten query. An unknown key raises <code>KeyError</code>. Constrain the schema or use <code>retrievers.get(key, default_retriever)</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@chain\ndef custom_chain(question: str):\n    structured_output = query_analyzer.invoke({&quot;question&quot;: question})\n    retriever = retrievers[structured_output.person]\n    return retriever.invoke(structured_output.query)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0083","card":"q-langchain-level-up-018-6-2-routing-to-a-retriever-with-a-custom","sec":"langchain-level-up-s019","q":"What is <code>RunnableBranch</code> and how does it relate to a custom routing function?","a":"<p><code>RunnableBranch</code> is a built-in way to route: you give condition and Runnable pairs plus a default, and it runs the first branch whose condition is true. A custom function with <code>@chain</code> does the same job with more flexibility, and in LangChain 0.2+ it is the approach the docs usually recommend for routing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0084","card":"q-langchain-level-up-018-6-2-routing-to-a-retriever-with-a-custom","sec":"langchain-level-up-s019","q":"Which steps happen in order when a routed Q&amp;A chain answers a question?","a":"<p>Analyze the question into structured output, select a retriever from that output, retrieve documents with the extracted query, then (for full RAG) pass the documents and question to the prompt, model and parser. Each step needs the output of the one before it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0085","card":"q-langchain-level-up-019-6-3-challenge-solution-shoes-vs-shirts-f","sec":"langchain-level-up-s020","q":"When you have two document sets, why create a separate vector store and retriever for each instead of one shared store?","a":"<p>Each query then searches only relevant content, giving higher precision and fewer irrelevant chunks, and you can tune each index separately. A shared store with a metadata filter is a valid alternative that avoids duplicate infrastructure. Pick separate stores when the sets differ in schema or access control, and a filter when they are similar.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0086","card":"q-langchain-level-up-019-6-3-challenge-solution-shoes-vs-shirts-f","sec":"langchain-level-up-s020","q":"In this query analyzer, why is <code>{\"question\": RunnablePassthrough()}</code> placed first?","a":"<p>The caller passes a plain string, but the prompt expects a dict with a <code>question</code> key. The dict with <code>RunnablePassthrough()</code> wraps the raw string into <code>{\"question\": &lt;string&gt;}</code> for the prompt.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>query_analyzer = {&quot;question&quot;: RunnablePassthrough()} | prompt_query | structured_llm</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0087","card":"q-langchain-level-up-019-6-3-challenge-solution-shoes-vs-shirts-f","sec":"langchain-level-up-s020","q":"How would you make a routed RAG chain return an answer instead of only documents?","a":"<p>Inside the routing function, build a RAG chain with the chosen retriever (<code>{\"context\": retriever, \"question\": RunnablePassthrough()} | prompt | llm | StrOutputParser()</code>) and invoke it with the original question. The retriever is chosen per request, so the chain is built per request too.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0088","card":"q-langchain-level-up-020-7-semantic-search-with-mongodb-atlas-vec","sec":"langchain-level-up-s021","q":"What is semantic search and how does it differ from keyword search?","a":"<p>Semantic search compares the meaning of the query and documents using embeddings, so \"how to protect my database\" matches text about security even without shared words. Keyword search matches terms, so it misses synonyms but is precise for exact terms, IDs and names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0089","card":"q-langchain-level-up-020-7-semantic-search-with-mongodb-atlas-vec","sec":"langchain-level-up-s021","q":"What is hybrid search and why use it?","a":"<p>Hybrid search combines semantic (vector) search with full-text keyword search and merges the results. It captures meaning and exact terms, which helps for queries with product names, codes or rare words that embeddings handle poorly. It costs more complexity in ranking and tuning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0090","card":"q-langchain-level-up-020-7-semantic-search-with-mongodb-atlas-vec","sec":"langchain-level-up-s021","q":"Why might you choose MongoDB Atlas Vector Search over a separate vector database?","a":"<p>Your documents and their embeddings live in the same database, so you avoid syncing two systems and can combine vector search with normal queries and filters. The drawback is that it ties you to Atlas, and a dedicated vector database can offer more specialized features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0091","card":"q-langchain-level-up-021-7-1-atlas-setup","sec":"langchain-level-up-s022","q":"Why must a MongoDB connection string with a username and password be kept in environment variables?","a":"<p>It contains credentials. Hard-coding it exposes the database through source control. Load it from <code>.env</code> with <code>os.getenv</code> and keep <code>.env</code> out of git.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0092","card":"q-langchain-level-up-021-7-1-atlas-setup","sec":"langchain-level-up-s022","q":"What does this code do, and what exception shows up if the credentials are wrong?","a":"<p><code>MongoClient</code> opens a client for the cluster, then the indexing syntax selects a database and a collection. <code>MongoClient</code> connects lazily, so a bad password usually fails on the first operation such as <code>list_database_names()</code>, with an authentication error from <code>pymongo.errors.OperationFailure</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from pymongo import MongoClient\n\nclient = MongoClient(ATLAS_CONNECTION_STRING)\ncollection = client[DB_NAME][COLLECTION_NAME]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0093","card":"q-langchain-level-up-021-7-1-atlas-setup","sec":"langchain-level-up-s022","q":"A new Atlas cluster cannot be reached from your machine even with the right string. What do you check?","a":"<p>Atlas blocks unknown IPs by default, so check the Network Access IP allowlist (add your current IP). Then confirm the database user and password, that special characters in the password are URL-encoded in the connection string, and that the user has the right role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0094","card":"q-langchain-level-up-021-7-1-atlas-setup","sec":"langchain-level-up-s022","q":"When Atlas does not accept a vector index creation, what permission may be missing?","a":"<p>Creating Atlas Search and Vector Search indexes needs sufficient privileges on the project or database user, for example the Atlas admin role as used in tutorials. Follow least privilege in production by granting only what is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0095","card":"q-langchain-level-up-022-7-2-load-data-and-create-the-vector-stor","sec":"langchain-level-up-s023","q":"What does <code>MongoDBAtlasVectorSearch.from_documents(documents, embeddings, collection=collection, index_name=...)</code> do?","a":"<p>It embeds each document with the embedding model, inserts the text, metadata and embedding into the MongoDB collection, and returns a vector store bound to that collection and the named search index. The index must exist for search to work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0096","card":"q-langchain-level-up-022-7-2-load-data-and-create-the-vector-stor","sec":"langchain-level-up-s023","q":"Why must <code>index_name</code> in Python match the name of the Atlas Vector Search index exactly?","a":"<p>Atlas looks up the index by name when running <code>$vectorSearch</code>. A different name means no index is found and searches fail or return nothing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0097","card":"q-langchain-level-up-022-7-2-load-data-and-create-the-vector-stor","sec":"langchain-level-up-s023","q":"In LangChain 0.1+, where do you import <code>MongoDBAtlasVectorSearch</code> from?","a":"<p>In 0.1/0.2 it was imported from <code>langchain_community.vectorstores</code>. Newer versions provide it in the dedicated <code>langchain-mongodb</code> package (<code>from langchain_mongodb import MongoDBAtlasVectorSearch</code>), which is the maintained location.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0098","card":"q-langchain-level-up-022-7-2-load-data-and-create-the-vector-stor","sec":"langchain-level-up-s023","q":"What does <code>PyPDFLoader</code> return, and what metadata does it keep?","a":"<p>A list of <code>Document</code> objects, one per PDF page, with the page text in <code>page_content</code> and the source and page number in <code>metadata</code>. That metadata lets you cite the page of an answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0099","card":"q-langchain-level-up-022-7-2-load-data-and-create-the-vector-stor","sec":"langchain-level-up-s023","q":"Which settings of <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=200, chunk_overlap=20)</code> would you reconsider for production RAG over PDFs, and why?","a":"<p>200 characters is very small and may cut context so chunks lose meaning. Typical RAG chunks are several hundred to a thousand characters. Tune chunk size and overlap against retrieval quality on real questions, not by guess.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0100","card":"q-langchain-level-up-023-7-3-create-the-atlas-vector-search-index","sec":"langchain-level-up-s024","q":"What does this Atlas Vector Search index definition configure?","a":"<p>It indexes the <code>embedding</code> field of each document as a vector of 1536 dimensions and ranks by cosine similarity. <code>path</code> is the document field holding the vector, <code>numDimensions</code> must equal the embedding model's output size, and <code>similarity</code> chooses the metric.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;fields&quot;: [\n    {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;vector&quot;,\n      &quot;path&quot;: &quot;embedding&quot;,\n      &quot;numDimensions&quot;: 1536,\n      &quot;similarity&quot;: &quot;cosine&quot;\n    }\n  ]\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0101","card":"q-langchain-level-up-023-7-3-create-the-atlas-vector-search-index","sec":"langchain-level-up-s024","q":"What breaks if <code>numDimensions</code> does not match the embedding model's output size?","a":"<p>Documents with a different vector length are not indexed correctly, and queries fail or return nothing. Whenever you change the embedding model, update the dimension and re-embed the data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0102","card":"q-langchain-level-up-023-7-3-create-the-atlas-vector-search-index","sec":"langchain-level-up-s024","q":"What similarity options does Atlas Vector Search support and how do you choose?","a":"<p><code>cosine</code>, <code>euclidean</code> and <code>dotProduct</code>. Cosine compares direction and is the common choice for text embeddings. Dot product suits normalized vectors and is faster. Euclidean uses distance. Follow the metric recommended for your embedding model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0103","card":"q-langchain-level-up-023-7-3-create-the-atlas-vector-search-index","sec":"langchain-level-up-s024","q":"Why does a search return nothing right after you create an Atlas Vector Search index?","a":"<p>The index is built asynchronously and must reach the Active state before queries work. Also check that the name matches the one in code, and the data has been inserted with embeddings in the indexed field.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0104","card":"q-langchain-level-up-023-7-3-create-the-atlas-vector-search-index","sec":"langchain-level-up-s024","q":"Why can you not simply query a normal MongoDB collection for similar vectors without a vector index?","a":"<p>A normal query cannot rank by vector similarity. Atlas Vector Search needs a dedicated index and the <code>$vectorSearch</code> aggregation stage (which LangChain calls for you). Without an index, the search would have to compare against every document.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0105","card":"q-langchain-level-up-024-7-4-similarity-search","sec":"langchain-level-up-s025","q":"What does <code>vector_store.similarity_search(\"MongoDB security\")</code> do and what does it return?","a":"<p>It embeds the query with the store's embedding model, asks the vector index for the nearest documents, and returns a list of <code>Document</code> objects, ordered by similarity. By default <code>k</code> is 4.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0106","card":"q-langchain-level-up-024-7-4-similarity-search","sec":"langchain-level-up-s025","q":"Why does <code>similarity_search</code> find a chunk about \"securing a cluster\" for the query \"protect my database\"?","a":"<p>Embeddings represent meaning, so the two phrases are close in vector space even though they share few words. That is the advantage over keyword matching.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0107","card":"q-langchain-level-up-024-7-4-similarity-search","sec":"langchain-level-up-s025","q":"What would you use if you also need the similarity score of each result?","a":"<p>Use <code>similarity_search_with_score</code>, which returns <code>(Document, score)</code> pairs. Scores let you apply a threshold and drop weak matches. Their scale depends on the metric and the store.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0108","card":"q-langchain-level-up-024-7-4-similarity-search","sec":"langchain-level-up-s025","q":"How can you restrict a vector search to a subset of documents, for example one document type?","a":"<p>Use a metadata pre-filter. In Atlas, the field must be declared as a <code>filter</code> field in the index, and then you pass a filter in the search call. It narrows the candidates before ranking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0109","card":"q-langchain-level-up-025-7-5-retrieval-rag-chain","sec":"langchain-level-up-s026","q":"What does <code>as_retriever(search_type=\"similarity\", search_kwargs={\"k\": 1})</code> configure?","a":"<p>It builds a retriever that does plain similarity search (cosine, as set in the index) and returns only the single best document. <code>k</code> controls how many documents reach the prompt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0110","card":"q-langchain-level-up-025-7-5-retrieval-rag-chain","sec":"langchain-level-up-s026","q":"Besides <code>similarity</code>, what other <code>search_type</code> values does a LangChain vector store retriever commonly support?","a":"<p><code>mmr</code> (maximal marginal relevance), which balances relevance with diversity to avoid near-duplicate chunks, and <code>similarity_score_threshold</code>, which returns only results that score over a set threshold. Availability depends on the vector store.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0111","card":"q-langchain-level-up-025-7-5-retrieval-rag-chain","sec":"langchain-level-up-s026","q":"Why wrap the chain call in a function such as <code>query_data(query)</code> that returns the answer?","a":"<p>It gives other code (a CLI, a web app, an API) a single entry point and hides chain details. The caller does not need to know about retrievers or prompts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0112","card":"q-langchain-level-up-026-8-agents-tools-over-mongodb-openai-tools","sec":"langchain-level-up-s027","q":"What is an LLM agent and how does it differ from a fixed chain?","a":"<p>An agent lets the LLM decide at run time which tool to call and with what arguments, loop on the results, and decide when to finish. A chain has a fixed sequence of steps. Agents are more flexible but less predictable, slower, and costlier because of multiple LLM calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0113","card":"q-langchain-level-up-026-8-agents-tools-over-mongodb-openai-tools","sec":"langchain-level-up-s027","q":"When would you use a RAG chain and when an agent with retrieval as a tool?","a":"<p>Use a plain chain when every question needs the same retrieval path: it is cheaper and predictable. Use an agent when questions may need different tools or data stores (a database, the web, a calculator) or several steps. An agent can also skip retrieval for questions that do not need it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0114","card":"q-langchain-level-up-026-8-agents-tools-over-mongodb-openai-tools","sec":"langchain-level-up-s027","q":"What risks come with agents that call tools?","a":"<p>The model may pick the wrong tool, pass bad arguments, loop, or run costly operations. Mitigate with clear tool descriptions, narrow tools with least privilege, a limit on iterations, input validation, and tracing. Tools that write data need extra care.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0115","card":"q-langchain-level-up-027-8-1-defining-tools","sec":"langchain-level-up-s028","q":"In LangChain, what does the <code>@tool</code> decorator do with a function?","a":"<p>It converts the function into a tool object. The function name becomes the tool name, the docstring becomes its description, and the type hints give the argument schema. All of that is passed to the model so it can decide when and how to call the tool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0116","card":"q-langchain-level-up-027-8-1-defining-tools","sec":"langchain-level-up-s028","q":"Why is the docstring mandatory on a function decorated with <code>@tool</code>?","a":"<p>The docstring is the description the LLM reads to decide when the tool applies. Without a description the decorator raises a validation error, and a vague one makes the model choose the wrong tool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0117","card":"q-langchain-level-up-027-8-1-defining-tools","sec":"langchain-level-up-s028","q":"What is wrong with this tool definition?","a":"<p>There is no docstring (so there is no description and the decorator raises an error), no type hints (so the argument schema is vague), and a name that says nothing about what is searched. Use a specific name, typed parameters and a clear docstring.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from langchain_core.tools import tool\n\n@tool\ndef search(q):\n    return db.find(q)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0118","card":"q-langchain-level-up-027-8-1-defining-tools","sec":"langchain-level-up-s028","q":"How would you expose an existing RAG chain to an agent as a tool?","a":"<p>Wrap a function that calls <code>rag_chain.invoke(query)</code> and returns the answer with <code>@tool</code>, with a docstring that describes which documents it covers. The agent then calls it like any tool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0119","card":"q-langchain-level-up-027-8-1-defining-tools","sec":"langchain-level-up-s028","q":"What is the trade-off of wrapping a full RAG chain as a tool compared with returning raw retrieved documents?","a":"<p>A full-chain tool returns a ready answer and uses one extra LLM call inside the tool, so cost and latency go up and the agent cannot reason over the raw evidence. A retriever-only tool lets the agent see the text itself and combine it with other tools, but the agent's own prompt carries more tokens.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0120","card":"q-langchain-level-up-028-8-2-agent-prompt-bind-tools-build-the-ag","sec":"langchain-level-up-s029","q":"What is <code>agent_scratchpad</code> in an agent prompt?","a":"<p>A placeholder (<code>MessagesPlaceholder(\"agent_scratchpad\")</code>) where the agent's intermediate steps go: the tool calls it made and the results. On each loop the model sees them, which allows multi-step reasoning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0121","card":"q-langchain-level-up-028-8-2-agent-prompt-bind-tools-build-the-ag","sec":"langchain-level-up-s029","q":"What does <code>llm.bind_tools(tools)</code> do?","a":"<p>It returns a copy of the model that sends the tool definitions (names, descriptions, argument schemas) with every request, so the model can respond with tool calls. It requires a model that supports tool calling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0122","card":"q-langchain-level-up-028-8-2-agent-prompt-bind-tools-build-the-ag","sec":"langchain-level-up-s029","q":"Why does the agent pipeline need <code>format_to_openai_tool_messages</code> and <code>OpenAIToolsAgentOutputParser</code>?","a":"<p>The formatter converts the list of <code>(action, observation)</code> steps into the message format OpenAI expects for tool calls and results, so they fill the scratchpad. The output parser turns the model's reply into either an <code>AgentAction</code> (call a tool) or an <code>AgentFinish</code> (final answer), which the executor can act on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0123","card":"q-langchain-level-up-028-8-2-agent-prompt-bind-tools-build-the-ag","sec":"langchain-level-up-s029","q":"In LangChain 0.1/0.2+, what is a shorter way to build this OpenAI-tools agent than assembling the pipeline by hand?","a":"<p><code>create_openai_tools_agent(llm, tools, prompt)</code> from <code>langchain.agents</code> builds the same agent runnable. The prompt must include the <code>agent_scratchpad</code> placeholder. In LangChain 1.0+, the recommended approach is <code>create_agent</code> (built on LangGraph), and the older <code>AgentExecutor</code> classes moved to the <code>langchain-classic</code> package.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0124","card":"q-langchain-level-up-028-8-2-agent-prompt-bind-tools-build-the-ag","sec":"langchain-level-up-s029","q":"What is <code>tool_choice</code>/forcing a tool and when would you use it?","a":"<p>Many chat models let you require that a specific tool is called (via the <code>tool_choice</code> option when binding tools). Use it when a step must always call a tool, for example to force structured output. For an open-ended agent, leave the model free to decide.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0125","card":"q-langchain-level-up-029-8-3-agentexecutor","sec":"langchain-level-up-s030","q":"What does <code>AgentExecutor</code> do?","a":"<p>It runs the agent loop: ask the agent what to do, if it returns an <code>AgentAction</code> call the tool and feed the result back as an observation, and repeat until the agent returns an <code>AgentFinish</code>. It then returns the final output.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0126","card":"q-langchain-level-up-029-8-3-agentexecutor","sec":"langchain-level-up-s030","q":"What does <code>verbose=True</code> do on <code>AgentExecutor</code> and why is it useful?","a":"<p>It prints each step: the tool the agent chose, its input, and the observation. It is useful for debugging tool selection. For production, use tracing (LangSmith) instead of console prints.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0127","card":"q-langchain-level-up-029-8-3-agentexecutor","sec":"langchain-level-up-s030","q":"What keys does <code>agent_executor.invoke({\"input\": \"...\"})</code> return?","a":"<p>A dict with <code>input</code> and <code>output</code>. The final answer is in <code>output</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0128","card":"q-langchain-level-up-029-8-3-agentexecutor","sec":"langchain-level-up-s030","q":"How do you stop an agent from looping forever or running up cost?","a":"<p>Set <code>max_iterations</code> (and optionally <code>max_execution_time</code>) on <code>AgentExecutor</code>, and use <code>handle_parsing_errors</code> so malformed model output does not crash it. Also write specific tool descriptions so the agent does not wander.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0129","card":"q-langchain-level-up-029-8-3-agentexecutor","sec":"langchain-level-up-s030","q":"How would you see which tool calls and intermediate results an agent made, to debug a wrong answer?","a":"<p>Create <code>AgentExecutor</code> with <code>return_intermediate_steps=True</code> to get the steps in the result, use <code>verbose=True</code> for console logs, or use a tracing platform such as LangSmith to inspect every LLM call and tool call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0130","card":"q-langchain-level-up-030-8-4-multitask-agent-challenge-solution","sec":"langchain-level-up-s031","q":"An agent over a PDF-backed vector search says \"I don't know\" for a how-to question. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>The data does not cover the question, so add a second tool backed by a source that does (for example documentation pages loaded with <code>WebBaseLoader</code>), give it a clear docstring, and add it to the tools list. The agent then chooses between tools from their descriptions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0131","card":"q-langchain-level-up-030-8-4-multitask-agent-challenge-solution","sec":"langchain-level-up-s031","q":"How does an agent decide which of several tools to call?","a":"<p>The model reads the tool names and descriptions (from docstrings) plus the user question and emits a tool call. There is no hidden router, so distinct, specific descriptions are the main way to guide it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0132","card":"q-langchain-level-up-030-8-4-multitask-agent-challenge-solution","sec":"langchain-level-up-s031","q":"What do you do when two tools have overlapping descriptions and the agent picks the wrong one?","a":"<p>Rewrite the docstrings so each states the data it covers and when to use it, add that guidance to the system prompt, and consider merging the tools if they are not really different. Test with real questions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0133","card":"q-langchain-level-up-030-8-4-multitask-agent-challenge-solution","sec":"langchain-level-up-s031","q":"What does <code>hub.pull(\"rlm/rag-prompt\")</code> do?","a":"<p>It downloads a prompt template from LangChain Hub, the shared prompt repository. It makes a network call at run time, so for production consider copying the prompt into your code to pin its version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0134","card":"q-langchain-level-up-031-10-deploying-chains-as-a-rest-api-with-l","sec":"langchain-level-up-s033","q":"What is LangServe and what does it give you?","a":"<p>LangServe is a library that exposes LangChain Runnables and chains as a REST API using FastAPI and Pydantic. It adds <code>invoke</code>, <code>batch</code>, <code>stream</code> and <code>stream_log</code> endpoints, input and output schemas, and a playground and Swagger docs. In newer LangChain guidance, LangServe is in maintenance mode, and LangGraph Platform is recommended for new deployments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0135","card":"q-langchain-level-up-031-10-deploying-chains-as-a-rest-api-with-l","sec":"langchain-level-up-s033","q":"Why does LangServe build on FastAPI and Pydantic?","a":"<p>FastAPI provides the web server with async support and automatic OpenAPI/Swagger docs. Pydantic validates the request and response schemas inferred from the Runnable's input and output types.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0136","card":"q-langchain-level-up-031-10-deploying-chains-as-a-rest-api-with-l","sec":"langchain-level-up-s033","q":"What is <code>uvicorn</code> and why do you need it?","a":"<p>Uvicorn is an ASGI server. FastAPI defines the app, but something must listen on a port and run it. Uvicorn does that (for example <code>uvicorn server:app --port 8000</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0137","card":"q-langchain-level-up-031-10-deploying-chains-as-a-rest-api-with-l","sec":"langchain-level-up-s033","q":"Compare calling a chain from a Python function with serving it through LangServe.","a":"<p>A function call works only inside that Python process. LangServe lets any client (web, mobile, other services) call it over HTTP, with schema validation, streaming and docs, and lets you scale and deploy the chain separately from the UI. The costs are a server to run, auth to add, and network latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0138","card":"q-langchain-level-up-032-10-1-server-routes-run","sec":"langchain-level-up-s034","q":"What does <code>add_routes(app, runnable, path=\"/joke\")</code> do?","a":"<p>It registers a set of endpoints for the Runnable under <code>/joke</code>: <code>POST /joke/invoke</code>, <code>/joke/batch</code>, <code>/joke/stream</code>, <code>/joke/stream_log</code>, plus a playground and schema routes. Each call adds one runnable under its own path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0139","card":"q-langchain-level-up-032-10-1-server-routes-run","sec":"langchain-level-up-s034","q":"What is the request body for a LangServe <code>invoke</code> endpoint for a chain whose prompt has a <code>{topic}</code> variable?","a":"<p>JSON of the form <code>{\"input\": {\"topic\": \"cats\"}}</code>. The <code>input</code> key holds the value passed to the runnable, which for a prompt-first chain is a dict of template variables. For a chain that takes a string, <code>input</code> is the string. The reply has an <code>output</code> field.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0140","card":"q-langchain-level-up-032-10-1-server-routes-run","sec":"langchain-level-up-s034","q":"How do <code>invoke</code>, <code>batch</code> and <code>stream</code> endpoints differ?","a":"<p><code>invoke</code> handles one input and returns the full output. <code>batch</code> takes a list of inputs and returns a list of outputs. <code>stream</code> returns output incrementally (server-sent events), so a UI can show tokens as they arrive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0141","card":"q-langchain-level-up-032-10-1-server-routes-run","sec":"langchain-level-up-s034","q":"What is the difference between <code>uvicorn server:app</code> and <code>uvicorn server:app --reload</code>?","a":"<p><code>--reload</code> watches the files and restarts the server on every change, which is handy for development. It uses extra CPU, so do not use it in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0142","card":"q-langchain-level-up-032-10-1-server-routes-run","sec":"langchain-level-up-s034","q":"What does the <code>if __name__ == \"__main__\": uvicorn.run(app, host=\"localhost\", port=8000)</code> block allow?","a":"<p>It lets you start the server with <code>python server.py</code>. It is an alternative to the <code>uvicorn</code> command line. When the file is imported by another process, the block does not run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0143","card":"q-langchain-level-up-033-10-2-challenge-solution-serve-the-atlas","sec":"langchain-level-up-s035","q":"How do you serve an existing RAG chain from a LangServe app?","a":"<p>Import the chain and call <code>add_routes(app, rag_chain, path=\"/query\")</code>. Make sure its dependencies (API keys, database connection) are available as environment variables on the server. Clients then post <code>{\"input\": \"question\"}</code> to <code>/query/invoke</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0144","card":"q-langchain-level-up-033-10-2-challenge-solution-serve-the-atlas","sec":"langchain-level-up-s035","q":"How would you call a LangServe endpoint from Python without hand-writing HTTP?","a":"<p>Use <code>requests.post(url, json={\"input\": ...})</code> and read <code>response.json()[\"output\"]</code>, or use <code>langserve.RemoteRunnable(url)</code>, which lets you call <code>invoke</code>, <code>batch</code> and <code>stream</code> on a remote chain as if it were local.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0145","card":"q-langchain-level-up-033-10-2-challenge-solution-serve-the-atlas","sec":"langchain-level-up-s035","q":"What do you need to add before exposing a LangServe RAG endpoint to the public internet?","a":"<p>Authentication and authorization, rate limiting, input validation, and CORS settings for browser clients. Without them anyone can spend your LLM credits and query your data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0146","card":"q-langchain-level-up-034-11-deploying-to-the-cloud-with-render","sec":"langchain-level-up-s036","q":"Why is <code>--host 0.0.0.0</code> used in the start command for a hosted web service, while <code>localhost</code> is fine locally?","a":"<p><code>localhost</code> accepts connections only from the same machine. Inside a container on a host platform, traffic comes from outside the container, so the server must listen on all interfaces. Binding <code>0.0.0.0</code> allows that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0147","card":"q-langchain-level-up-034-11-deploying-to-the-cloud-with-render","sec":"langchain-level-up-s036","q":"Where do you put the OpenAI key and database connection string for a Render web service when <code>.env</code> is not in the repository?","a":"<p>In the service's environment variables in the platform dashboard. The app reads them with <code>os.getenv</code> the same way as locally. This keeps secrets out of git.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0148","card":"q-langchain-level-up-034-11-deploying-to-the-cloud-with-render","sec":"langchain-level-up-s036","q":"What should a <code>.gitignore</code> for a LangChain web service contain and why?","a":"<p><code>.env</code> (secrets), <code>__pycache__/</code> and <code>*.pyc</code> (build artifacts), and the virtual environment folder such as <code>.venv/</code>. These files are machine-specific or sensitive and should not be in the repository.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0149","card":"q-langchain-level-up-034-11-deploying-to-the-cloud-with-render","sec":"langchain-level-up-s036","q":"Render injects a <code>PORT</code> environment variable. How should the start command respect it?","a":"<p>Hosting platforms may assign the port. Render's web services listen on the port given in <code>PORT</code> (by default 10000), so a robust start command is <code>uvicorn server:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port $PORT</code>. Check the platform docs for the current default and behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0150","card":"q-langchain-level-up-034-11-deploying-to-the-cloud-with-render","sec":"langchain-level-up-s036","q":"A deploy on a free hosting tier works but the first request after idle time is slow. Why?","a":"<p>Free web services are commonly spun down after inactivity and need a cold start on the next request. Use a paid instance, or accept the cold start for prototypes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0151","card":"q-langchain-level-up-034-11-deploying-to-the-cloud-with-render","sec":"langchain-level-up-s036","q":"A deployed service fails with <code>ModuleNotFoundError</code> though it works locally. What do you check?","a":"<p>Whether the package is listed in <code>requirements.txt</code>, because the platform installs only what is listed there. Also check the Python version, and that pinned versions are compatible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0152","card":"q-langchain-level-up-035-12-course-recap-core-components","sec":"langchain-level-up-s037","q":"In a LangChain RAG app, which component handles chunking, which embedding, which storage and which retrieval?","a":"<p>A text splitter such as <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code> chunks, an embeddings class such as <code>OpenAIEmbeddings</code> embeds, a vector store such as Chroma, FAISS or MongoDB Atlas Vector Search stores vectors, and <code>as_retriever()</code> provides retrieval for chains.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-level-up-f0153","card":"q-langchain-level-up-035-12-course-recap-core-components","sec":"langchain-level-up-s037","q":"How would you decide between FAISS, Chroma and a managed vector search such as MongoDB Atlas for a project?","a":"<p>FAISS for quick in-memory prototypes, Chroma for a simple local store with persistence and metadata, and a managed service when you need durability, scaling, security controls, filters, and your data already lives there. The choice depends on size, ops effort and where the source data lives.</p>","hand":true}];
